- The tracking maths in public/js/track/ and the rig simulator in public/js/sim/ are original code, written
  from published algorithms rather than copied from any implementation: the normalised 8-point essential
  matrix and its four-fold decomposition (Hartley & Zisserman, Multiple View Geometry), Umeyama's
  least-squares similarity fit (IEEE PAMI 1991), the 1 euro filter (Casiez, Roussel & Vogel, CHI 2012) and
  Kooima's generalised perspective projection for the off-axis frustum. No third-party source is included.

- The pinch thresholds in public/js/track/landmarks.js, public/js/input/cameras.js and
  public/js/interact/config.js (25 mm to close, 30 mm to open, with hysteresis) are the published defaults
  of Ultraleap's PinchDetector, from the Ultraleap Unity Plugin (https://github.com/ultraleap/UnityPlugin),
  Apache License 2.0, Copyright Ultraleap Ltd. They are cited parameters, not code; no Ultraleap source is
  used.

- The ZED calibration file format read by parseZedConf() in public/js/track/calibrate.js is documented by
  Stereolabs (https://support.stereolabs.com), and the file itself is data Stereolabs supplies per camera
  serial. No part of the proprietary ZED SDK is used or linked: the optional bridge is a separate process
  that sends plain numbers over a socket, so this GPL-3.0 code never links against it.
- tools/zed-bridge/ is a standalone helper program, not part of the app. When it is run against a real
  camera it imports the Stereolabs ZED SDK Python bindings (pyzed) and, in its default mode, MediaPipe's
  Python package (Apache License 2.0). The ZED SDK is proprietary and is NOT distributed here; the bridge
  runs as a separate process and talks to the app only over a WebSocket, so no GPL-3.0 code in this
  repository links against it. Its "--fake" mode uses nothing but the Python standard library.
